WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:16.256 --> 00:22.196 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference October 29,

00:22.876 --> 00:25.066 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:25.066 --> 00:25.386 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:25.386 --> 00:27.876 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:27.876 --> 00:31.546 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:31.546 --> 00:34.256 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:35.556 --> 00:37.936 align:middle
Although some important federal
government data have been

00:37.936 --> 00:41.546 align:middle
delayed due to the shutdown, the
public- and private-sector data

00:41.546 --> 00:44.256 align:middle
that have remained available
suggest that the outlook

00:44.256 --> 00:47.456 align:middle
for employment and
inflation has not changed much

00:47.556 --> 00:48.836 align:middle
since our meeting in September.

00:49.926 --> 00:52.646 align:middle
Conditions in the labor market
appear to be gradually cooling,

00:53.116 --> 00:55.306 align:middle
and inflation remains
somewhat elevated.

00:55.996 --> 00:59.506 align:middle
In support of our goals, and in
light of the balance of risks

00:59.506 --> 01:00.776 align:middle
to employment and inflation,

01:01.226 --> 01:04.066 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

01:04.066 --> 01:07.686 align:middle
to lower our policy interest
rate by ¼ percentage point.

01:08.596 --> 01:10.596 align:middle
We also decided to
conclude the reduction

01:10.596 --> 01:13.516 align:middle
of our aggregate securities
holdings as of December 1.

01:14.406 --> 01:16.806 align:middle
I will have more to say
about monetary policy

01:16.806 --> 01:19.216 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:21.126 --> 01:22.526 align:middle
Available indicators suggest

01:22.526 --> 01:25.776 align:middle
that economic activity has been
expanding at a moderate pace.

01:26.306 --> 01:30.346 align:middle
GDP rose at a 1.6 percent pace
in the first half of the year,

01:30.936 --> 01:33.056 align:middle
down from 2.4 percent last year.

01:34.096 --> 01:37.126 align:middle
Data available prior to the
shutdown show that growth

01:37.126 --> 01:40.626 align:middle
in economic activity may be on
a somewhat firmer trajectory

01:40.626 --> 01:43.576 align:middle
than expected, primarily
reflecting stronger

01:43.576 --> 01:44.486 align:middle
consumer spending.

01:45.936 --> 01:48.606 align:middle
Business investment in equipment
and intangibles has continued

01:48.606 --> 01:52.486 align:middle
to expand, while activity in
the housing sector remains weak.

01:53.846 --> 01:55.776 align:middle
The shutdown of the federal
government will weigh

01:55.776 --> 01:58.006 align:middle
on economic activity
while it persists,

01:58.486 --> 02:01.706 align:middle
but these effects should
reverse after the shutdown ends.

02:02.256 --> 02:04.246 align:middle
In the labor market,

02:04.246 --> 02:07.266 align:middle
the unemployment rate remained
relatively low through August.

02:08.256 --> 02:10.246 align:middle
Job gains have slowed
significantly

02:10.246 --> 02:11.986 align:middle
since earlier in the year.

02:12.766 --> 02:15.296 align:middle
A good part of the slowing
likely reflects a decline

02:15.296 --> 02:18.636 align:middle
in the growth of the labor
force due to lower immigration

02:18.636 --> 02:20.176 align:middle
and labor force participation,

02:20.666 --> 02:23.346 align:middle
though labor demand has
clearly softened as well.

02:24.756 --> 02:27.516 align:middle
Although official employment
data for September are delayed,

02:28.096 --> 02:30.426 align:middle
available evidence
suggests that both layoffs

02:30.476 --> 02:34.176 align:middle
and hiring remain low, and that
both households' perceptions

02:34.176 --> 02:37.056 align:middle
of job availability
and firms' perceptions

02:37.056 --> 02:39.606 align:middle
of hiring difficulty
continue to decline.

02:40.216 --> 02:44.226 align:middle
In this less dynamic and
somewhat softer labor market,

02:44.406 --> 02:46.226 align:middle
the downside risks
to employment appear

02:46.226 --> 02:48.426 align:middle
to have risen in recent months.

02:49.296 --> 02:51.456 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

02:51.456 --> 02:55.296 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

02:55.366 --> 02:57.466 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:58.926 --> 03:01.966 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index suggest

03:01.966 --> 03:05.096 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.8 percent

03:05.126 --> 03:08.796 align:middle
over the 12 months ending
in September and that,

03:08.796 --> 03:11.096 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:11.586 --> 03:14.816 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.8 percent as well.

03:15.996 --> 03:18.256 align:middle
These readings are higher
than earlier in the year,

03:18.496 --> 03:20.386 align:middle
as inflation for
goods has picked up.

03:21.166 --> 03:23.476 align:middle
In contrast, disinflation
appears

03:23.476 --> 03:25.086 align:middle
to be continuing for services.

03:25.696 --> 03:28.486 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have moved up,

03:28.486 --> 03:31.286 align:middle
on balance, over the course of
this year on news about tariffs,

03:31.826 --> 03:34.716 align:middle
as reflected in both market-
and survey-based measures.

03:35.666 --> 03:40.106 align:middle
Beyond the next year or
so, however, most measures

03:40.106 --> 03:42.336 align:middle
of longer-term expectations
remain consistent

03:42.336 --> 03:44.366 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:44.896 --> 03:50.186 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:50.186 --> 03:52.876 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:52.876 --> 03:53.926 align:middle
for the American people.

03:55.146 --> 03:56.846 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:56.846 --> 03:59.116 align:middle
to lower the target range
for the federal funds rate

03:59.566 --> 04:03.906 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point
to 3  to 4 percent.

04:05.506 --> 04:08.106 align:middle
Higher tariffs are pushing
up prices in some categories

04:08.106 --> 04:11.176 align:middle
of goods, resulting in
higher overall inflation.

04:12.086 --> 04:14.586 align:middle
A reasonable base case
is that the effects

04:14.586 --> 04:17.966 align:middle
on inflation will be relatively
short lived-a one-time shift

04:18.206 --> 04:18.936 align:middle
in the price level.

04:20.076 --> 04:23.186 align:middle
But it is also possible that
the inflationary effects could

04:23.186 --> 04:26.006 align:middle
instead be more persistent,
and that is a risk

04:26.036 --> 04:27.566 align:middle
to be assessed and managed.

04:28.306 --> 04:31.286 align:middle
Our obligation is to ensure
that a one-time increase

04:31.286 --> 04:33.506 align:middle
in the price level does
not become an ongoing

04:33.576 --> 04:34.416 align:middle
inflation problem.

04:34.916 --> 04:38.826 align:middle
In the near term, risks
to inflation are tilted

04:38.826 --> 04:41.296 align:middle
to the upside and
risks to employment

04:41.386 --> 04:44.276 align:middle
to the downside-a
challenging situation.

04:45.126 --> 04:47.426 align:middle
There is no risk-free
path for policy

04:47.426 --> 04:48.796 align:middle
as we navigate this tension

04:48.796 --> 04:51.126 align:middle
between our employment
and inflation goals.

04:52.016 --> 04:54.466 align:middle
Our framework calls for us
to take a balanced approach

04:54.466 --> 04:56.856 align:middle
in promoting both sides
of our dual mandate.

04:58.146 --> 05:00.416 align:middle
With downside risks to
employment having increased

05:00.416 --> 05:02.816 align:middle
in recent months, the
balance of risks has shifted.

05:03.416 --> 05:05.786 align:middle
Accordingly, we judged it
appropriate at this meeting

05:05.786 --> 05:08.676 align:middle
to take another step toward
a more neutral policy stance.

05:10.856 --> 05:14.066 align:middle
With today's decision, we remain
well positioned to respond

05:14.066 --> 05:16.996 align:middle
in a timely way to potential
economic developments.

05:17.816 --> 05:20.716 align:middle
We will continue to determine
the appropriate stance

05:20.716 --> 05:23.476 align:middle
of monetary policy based
on the incoming data,

05:23.656 --> 05:26.566 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

05:26.996 --> 05:29.236 align:middle
We continue to face
two-sided risks.

05:30.586 --> 05:32.616 align:middle
In the Committee's
discussions at this meeting,

05:32.896 --> 05:35.466 align:middle
there were strongly
differing views about how

05:35.466 --> 05:36.766 align:middle
to proceed in December.

05:37.206 --> 05:39.806 align:middle
A further reduction
in the policy rate

05:39.896 --> 05:44.226 align:middle
at the December meeting is not a
foregone conclusion-far from it.

05:45.416 --> 05:47.476 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

05:48.036 --> 05:51.246 align:middle
At today's meeting, the
Committee also decided

05:51.246 --> 05:52.466 align:middle
to conclude the reduction

05:52.466 --> 05:55.296 align:middle
of our aggregate securities
holdings as of December 1.

05:56.556 --> 05:59.526 align:middle
Our long-stated plan has been
to stop balance sheet runoff

05:59.526 --> 06:02.416 align:middle
when reserves are somewhat above
the level we judge consistent

06:02.416 --> 06:03.756 align:middle
with ample reserve conditions.

06:04.926 --> 06:07.886 align:middle
Signs have clearly emerged that
we have reached that standard.

06:08.516 --> 06:12.026 align:middle
In money markets, repo
rates have moved up relative

06:12.026 --> 06:13.176 align:middle
to our administered rates,

06:13.346 --> 06:15.066 align:middle
and we have seen more
notable pressures

06:15.066 --> 06:17.276 align:middle
on selected dates
along with more use

06:17.276 --> 06:18.756 align:middle
of our standing repo facility.

06:20.126 --> 06:23.056 align:middle
In addition, the effective
federal funds rate has begun

06:23.056 --> 06:26.016 align:middle
to move up relative to the rate
of interest on reserve balances.

06:26.786 --> 06:29.996 align:middle
These developments are what
we expected to see as the size

06:29.996 --> 06:31.216 align:middle
of our balance sheet declined

06:32.116 --> 06:34.456 align:middle
and warrant today's
decision to cease runoff.

06:34.986 --> 06:37.496 align:middle
Over the three and a half years

06:37.496 --> 06:39.066 align:middle
that we have been
shrinking our balance sheet,

06:39.296 --> 06:42.766 align:middle
our securities holdings have
declined by $2.2 trillion.

06:43.626 --> 06:46.706 align:middle
As a share of nominal GDP,
our balance sheet has fallen

06:46.706 --> 06:49.156 align:middle
from 35 percent to
about 21 percent.

06:50.106 --> 06:51.776 align:middle
In December, we will
enter the next phase

06:51.776 --> 06:54.026 align:middle
of our normalization
plans by holding the size

06:54.026 --> 06:55.106 align:middle
of our balance sheet steady

06:55.106 --> 06:57.906 align:middle
for a time while
reserve balances continue

06:57.906 --> 07:01.036 align:middle
to move gradually lower as
other nonreserve liabilities

07:01.036 --> 07:02.656 align:middle
such as currency keep growing.

07:03.996 --> 07:06.306 align:middle
We will continue to
allow agency securities

07:06.306 --> 07:08.746 align:middle
to run off our balance sheet
and will reinvest the proceeds

07:08.746 --> 07:11.066 align:middle
from those securities
in Treasury bills,

07:11.636 --> 07:14.906 align:middle
furthering progress toward a
portfolio consisting primarily

07:14.906 --> 07:15.966 align:middle
of Treasury securities.

07:16.746 --> 07:19.746 align:middle
This reinvestment strategy will
also help move the weighted

07:19.746 --> 07:22.796 align:middle
average maturity of our
portfolio closer to that

07:22.796 --> 07:25.116 align:middle
of the outstanding stock
of Treasury securities,

07:25.706 --> 07:27.226 align:middle
thus furthering the
normalization

07:27.226 --> 07:29.156 align:middle
of the composition
of our balance sheet.

07:31.336 --> 07:33.346 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

07:33.346 --> 07:36.736 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

07:37.296 --> 07:40.636 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

07:40.686 --> 07:43.056 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

07:43.586 --> 07:45.866 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

07:45.996 --> 07:46.576 align:middle
well anchored.

07:47.586 --> 07:48.706 align:middle
Our success in delivering

07:48.706 --> 07:50.836 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

07:51.596 --> 07:54.076 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

07:54.076 --> 07:55.726 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

07:56.836 --> 07:59.106 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:59.726 --> 08:01.456 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:01.516 --> 08:03.086 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:03.086 --> 08:04.296 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:04.976 --> 08:05.316 align:middle
Thank you.

08:05.426 --> 08:07.176 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

08:07.436 --> 08:08.556 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:09.496 --> 08:11.306 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

08:12.706 --> 08:15.156 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

08:15.476 --> 08:16.736 align:middle
Chair Powell, are
you uncomfortable

08:16.736 --> 08:20.746 align:middle
with how market pricing has
assumed a rate cut is a foregone

08:20.746 --> 08:22.066 align:middle
conclusion at your next meeting?

08:22.596 --> 08:22.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:23.366 --> 08:27.316 align:middle
Well, as I just mentioned,
a further reduction

08:27.316 --> 08:27.996 align:middle
to the policy rate

08:27.996 --> 08:29.966 align:middle
at the December meeting is
not a foregone conclusion,

08:30.136 --> 08:30.886 align:middle
as I've just said.

08:31.096 --> 08:34.586 align:middle
So I would say that that
needs to be taken on board.

08:35.126 --> 08:39.296 align:middle
We had-you know, I would
just say this-19 participants

08:39.296 --> 08:42.056 align:middle
on the Committee, everyone
works very hard at this

08:42.056 --> 08:43.246 align:middle
and takes their obligations

08:43.306 --> 08:45.206 align:middle
to serve the American
people very seriously.

08:45.826 --> 08:49.196 align:middle
And at a time when we have
tension between our two goals,

08:49.686 --> 08:53.106 align:middle
we have strong views
across the Committee.

08:53.106 --> 08:56.256 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, they were
strongly differing views today.

08:56.426 --> 08:59.656 align:middle
And the "takeaway" from that is
that we haven't made a decision

08:59.656 --> 09:02.556 align:middle
about December, and we're
going to be looking at the data

09:02.556 --> 09:05.506 align:middle
that we have, how that affects
the outlook and the balance

09:05.506 --> 09:08.016 align:middle
of risks, and I'll
just say that.

09:08.076 --> 09:08.676 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:08.676 --> 09:11.576 align:middle
You and some of your colleagues
have framed last month

09:11.666 --> 09:13.856 align:middle
and maybe today-I
won't put words

09:13.856 --> 09:16.096 align:middle
in your mouth-as a
risk-management exercise.

09:16.346 --> 09:19.076 align:middle
At what point do you conclude

09:19.636 --> 09:21.476 align:middle
that you've taken
out enough insurance?

09:21.476 --> 09:24.886 align:middle
Are you looking for some kind
of improvement in the outlook?

09:25.246 --> 09:28.816 align:middle
Or could this unfold along
the lines of last year,

09:29.156 --> 09:31.866 align:middle
where you made a sequence
of adjustments and waited

09:32.216 --> 09:33.666 align:middle
to gather more information?

09:33.666 --> 09:34.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:34.686 --> 09:39.156 align:middle
So the way we have been
thinking-the way I've been

09:39.846 --> 09:41.616 align:middle
thinking about it is the risks

09:41.616 --> 09:45.006 align:middle
to the two goals-for a very
long time, the risk was clearly

09:45.006 --> 09:48.376 align:middle
of higher inflation, and
then that has changed now.

09:48.886 --> 09:50.646 align:middle
And as we saw the-particularly

09:50.646 --> 09:52.476 align:middle
after we saw-after
the July meeting,

09:52.476 --> 09:54.926 align:middle
we saw downward revisions
in job creation,

09:55.446 --> 09:57.576 align:middle
we saw a very different
picture of the labor market

09:57.576 --> 10:00.596 align:middle
and suggested that there
were higher downside risks

10:00.636 --> 10:02.206 align:middle
to the labor market
than we had thought.

10:02.776 --> 10:06.756 align:middle
And that suggested that policy,
which we had been holding at a,

10:07.106 --> 10:09.016 align:middle
I would say, "modestly"-other
people would say

10:09.016 --> 10:11.996 align:middle
"moderately"-restrictive
level, needed to move more

10:11.996 --> 10:14.546 align:middle
in the direction,
over time, of neutral.

10:14.966 --> 10:18.176 align:middle
If the two goals are sort of
equally at risk, then you ought

10:18.176 --> 10:20.726 align:middle
to be at neutral, because one of
them is calling for you to hike,

10:20.726 --> 10:22.246 align:middle
and one of them is
calling for you to cut.

10:22.716 --> 10:25.696 align:middle
So if that got back into
balance, then you'd want

10:25.696 --> 10:26.546 align:middle
to be roughly at neutral.

10:26.956 --> 10:29.026 align:middle
So in that sense, it was a
[case of] risk management,

10:29.576 --> 10:33.496 align:middle
and I would say the same about
today-sort of the same logic.

10:33.696 --> 10:36.766 align:middle
But as I mentioned, going
forward is a different thing.

10:37.956 --> 10:38.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:39.516 --> 10:40.956 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

10:42.016 --> 10:43.676 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

10:44.706 --> 10:49.426 align:middle
Thank you for taking
this question.

10:49.526 --> 10:53.486 align:middle
We've just heard from you that
the discussion in December

10:53.486 --> 10:55.156 align:middle
and the conclusion that
the discussion is not a

10:55.156 --> 10:56.396 align:middle
foregone conclusion.

10:56.786 --> 10:59.796 align:middle
I'd like to just dig into that a
little bit more, about what sort

10:59.796 --> 11:01.196 align:middle
of arguments were brought up.

11:01.196 --> 11:03.386 align:middle
Was there any consideration,
for instance,

11:03.386 --> 11:08.946 align:middle
of the investment we're seeing
in AI and some of the generation

11:08.946 --> 11:10.526 align:middle
of household wealth
through rises

11:10.526 --> 11:14.426 align:middle
in stock prices related
to the AI boom?

11:14.706 --> 11:15.686 align:middle
Thank you.

11:15.686 --> 11:15.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:15.906 --> 11:18.256 align:middle
I wouldn't say that's
a-that's a factor

11:18.506 --> 11:19.956 align:middle
in everyone's assessment
of the economy.

11:19.956 --> 11:21.626 align:middle
I wouldn't say it's
a driving factor,

11:22.106 --> 11:23.086 align:middle
I don't think, for anybody.

11:23.326 --> 11:27.236 align:middle
I guess I would say it this way:
Once again, I would just point

11:27.236 --> 11:30.246 align:middle
out that we have the
situation where the risks are

11:30.246 --> 11:33.566 align:middle
to the upside for inflation and
to the downside for employment.

11:34.466 --> 11:35.266 align:middle
We have one tool.

11:35.496 --> 11:37.316 align:middle
It can't do both of
those-you can't address both

11:37.316 --> 11:37.916 align:middle
of those at once.

11:37.916 --> 11:39.176 align:middle
You've got a very
different situation.

11:39.176 --> 11:42.606 align:middle
So you have some people-people
have different forecasts, right?

11:42.676 --> 11:46.796 align:middle
So they'll feel-they'll
forecast faster

11:46.796 --> 11:48.316 align:middle
or slower progress
on one or the other.

11:48.496 --> 11:50.476 align:middle
And they also have different
levels of risk aversion.

11:50.686 --> 11:56.556 align:middle
And people-some will be more
averse to inflation overruns,

11:56.556 --> 11:59.556 align:middle
and some will be more averse
to underruns of employment.

11:59.956 --> 12:03.666 align:middle
And so you put that together,
and as you can see from the SEP

12:03.666 --> 12:05.626 align:middle
and from the public
discussion that goes

12:05.626 --> 12:09.686 align:middle
on between the meetings when
participants go out and talk,

12:09.976 --> 12:11.186 align:middle
they're very disparate views.

12:11.326 --> 12:13.646 align:middle
And they were reflected in
strongly differing views

12:13.646 --> 12:16.286 align:middle
in today's meeting, as I
pointed out in my remarks.

12:16.886 --> 12:18.516 align:middle
And that's what leads
me to say that,

12:19.066 --> 12:23.336 align:middle
that we haven't made a
decision about December.

12:23.336 --> 12:25.026 align:middle
You know, I always
say that it's a fact

12:25.026 --> 12:26.956 align:middle
that we don't make
decisions in advance.

12:26.956 --> 12:28.866 align:middle
But this is-I'm saying
something in addition here,

12:29.296 --> 12:31.536 align:middle
that it's not-it's
not to be seen

12:31.536 --> 12:33.496 align:middle
as a foregone conclusion-in
fact, far from it.

12:33.986 --> 12:34.616 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

12:34.666 --> 12:36.616 align:middle
Can I just ask you a
quick follow-up on QT?

12:36.616 --> 12:40.076 align:middle
How much of the fund
impressions we've seen

12:40.076 --> 12:41.426 align:middle
in money markets are related

12:41.426 --> 12:44.296 align:middle
to the U.S. Treasury
issuing more short-term debt?

12:44.886 --> 12:45.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:45.896 --> 12:48.956 align:middle
That could be one of the
factors, but the reality is

12:48.956 --> 12:52.946 align:middle
that we've seen-the things that
we've seen, higher repo rates

12:52.946 --> 12:54.226 align:middle
and the federal funds
rate moving up,

12:54.226 --> 12:57.086 align:middle
these are the very things
that we-that we look for.

12:57.086 --> 12:59.576 align:middle
We actually have a
framework for looking

12:59.576 --> 13:02.886 align:middle
at the place we're
trying to reach.

13:02.886 --> 13:06.726 align:middle
What we said for a long time
now is that when we feel

13:06.726 --> 13:09.206 align:middle
like we're a little bit or a bit

13:09.206 --> 13:11.796 align:middle
above what we consider
a level that's ample

13:12.286 --> 13:14.556 align:middle
that we would freeze the
size of the balance sheet.

13:14.676 --> 13:16.636 align:middle
Of course, reserves
will continue to decline

13:16.636 --> 13:20.256 align:middle
from that point forward as
nonreserve liabilities grow.

13:20.626 --> 13:22.486 align:middle
So this happened, some

13:22.486 --> 13:24.936 align:middle
of it-some things have been
happening for some time now,

13:24.936 --> 13:27.616 align:middle
showing a gradual tightening
in money market conditions.

13:27.976 --> 13:30.236 align:middle
Really, in the last, call
it, three weeks or so,

13:30.646 --> 13:32.726 align:middle
you've seen more
significant tightening

13:32.726 --> 13:36.356 align:middle
and I think a clear assessment
that we're at that place.

13:36.726 --> 13:39.916 align:middle
The other thing is, we're-the
balance sheet is shrinking

13:39.916 --> 13:41.936 align:middle
at a very, very slow pace now.

13:42.116 --> 13:46.986 align:middle
We've reduced it by half
twice, and so there's not a lot

13:46.986 --> 13:50.506 align:middle
of benefit to be-to
be holding on for it

13:50.506 --> 13:54.136 align:middle
to get the last few
dollars, because, again,

13:54.136 --> 13:56.186 align:middle
when the balance sheet-reserves
are going to continue

13:56.186 --> 13:57.926 align:middle
to shrink as nonreserves grow.

13:57.926 --> 14:01.086 align:middle
So there was support on the
Committee, as we thought

14:01.086 --> 14:02.286 align:middle
about it, to go ahead with this

14:02.286 --> 14:04.856 align:middle
and announce effective
December 1

14:04.856 --> 14:07.156 align:middle
that we will be freezing the
size of the balance sheet.

14:07.216 --> 14:10.336 align:middle
And the December 1 date gives
the markets a little bit

14:10.336 --> 14:11.236 align:middle
of time to adapt.

14:12.816 --> 14:13.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:13.156 --> 14:16.046 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

14:16.426 --> 14:16.956 align:middle
Thank you.

14:16.956 --> 14:18.256 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

14:18.746 --> 14:20.586 align:middle
So much of the rationale
for cutting interest rates,

14:20.696 --> 14:23.516 align:middle
even as inflation moves away
from the 2 percent target,

14:23.576 --> 14:26.206 align:middle
seems to be that there are
these mounting downside risks

14:26.366 --> 14:27.096 align:middle
to the labor market.

14:27.096 --> 14:29.366 align:middle
But if those don't materialize

14:29.856 --> 14:31.846 align:middle
and the labor market
either stabilizes

14:31.846 --> 14:33.976 align:middle
around current employment
levels or even starts

14:33.976 --> 14:35.316 align:middle
to strengthen somewhat,

14:35.806 --> 14:37.466 align:middle
how would that change
your perception

14:37.466 --> 14:39.806 align:middle
of how much interest rates
need to fall from here?

14:40.086 --> 14:42.116 align:middle
Would you then be a
bit more concerned

14:42.116 --> 14:44.856 align:middle
about underlying inflation
and the possibility

14:44.856 --> 14:46.526 align:middle
of second-round effects
from tariffs?

14:46.526 --> 14:46.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:46.996 --> 14:50.216 align:middle
Yeah, I mean in principle if you
were to see data that suggested

14:50.216 --> 14:51.796 align:middle
that the labor market
is strengthening or even

14:51.796 --> 14:54.656 align:middle
that it's stabilizing,
that would certainly play

14:54.656 --> 14:56.366 align:middle
into our decisions
going forward.

14:56.666 --> 14:58.866 align:middle
So, and we do have-we
get some data.

14:58.866 --> 15:01.536 align:middle
The labor market is a place
where we get-for example,

15:01.536 --> 15:03.816 align:middle
we get the state-level
data on initial claims,

15:04.186 --> 15:07.556 align:middle
which are sending sort of a
signal of more of the same.

15:07.856 --> 15:12.016 align:middle
We also get job openings, and
we'll get lots of survey data,

15:12.016 --> 15:13.476 align:middle
we'll get the Beige Book
and things like that.

15:13.476 --> 15:15.676 align:middle
So we'll have a-we'll have
a picture of what's going

15:15.676 --> 15:16.546 align:middle
on in the labor market.

15:17.256 --> 15:21.406 align:middle
And the fact that we're not
seeing an uptick in claims

15:21.626 --> 15:25.096 align:middle
or a downtick, really,
in openings suggests

15:25.096 --> 15:28.506 align:middle
that you're seeing maybe
continued very gradual cooling,

15:28.506 --> 15:29.516 align:middle
but nothing more than that.

15:29.916 --> 15:31.386 align:middle
So that does give
you some comfort.

15:32.056 --> 15:32.636 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

15:32.856 --> 15:35.206 align:middle
But if this shutdown lasts a
while longer and you don't have

15:35.206 --> 15:37.706 align:middle
that data in hand,
I'm just wondering how

15:37.916 --> 15:40.916 align:middle
that hinders the Committee's
ability to assess the state

15:40.916 --> 15:44.526 align:middle
of the labor market and make
the right policy decisions.

15:44.826 --> 15:46.566 align:middle
And, also, how much
is that factoring

15:46.596 --> 15:48.446 align:middle
into the debate about December?

15:48.606 --> 15:49.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:49.686 --> 15:53.656 align:middle
Yeah. So we'll-we get-I
mentioned what we get

15:53.656 --> 15:54.606 align:middle
in the labor area.

15:54.606 --> 15:58.626 align:middle
We get some data in inflation,
some data in economic activity.

15:59.136 --> 16:01.926 align:middle
And we'll get-we'll have a
picture of what's going on.

16:01.926 --> 16:03.476 align:middle
We also will have
the Beige Book again.

16:04.036 --> 16:06.836 align:middle
I would say we're
not going to be able

16:06.836 --> 16:10.446 align:middle
to have the detailed
feel of things,

16:10.556 --> 16:14.456 align:middle
but I think if there were
significant or material change

16:14.776 --> 16:16.336 align:middle
in the economy one
way or another,

16:16.336 --> 16:18.876 align:middle
I think we'd pick
that up through this.

16:19.436 --> 16:22.096 align:middle
So in terms of how it
might affect December,

16:22.096 --> 16:22.976 align:middle
it's really hard to say.

16:22.976 --> 16:26.026 align:middle
December is a-the meeting's,
like, six weeks away.

16:26.026 --> 16:29.696 align:middle
We just don't know what
we're going to get.

16:29.856 --> 16:32.346 align:middle
If there is a very high
level of uncertainty,

16:32.346 --> 16:35.526 align:middle
then that could be
an argument in favor

16:35.526 --> 16:36.676 align:middle
of caution about moving.

16:37.226 --> 16:38.676 align:middle
But we'll have to
see how it unfolds.

16:40.236 --> 16:41.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:42.376 --> 16:43.646 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

16:43.686 --> 16:44.636 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

16:44.846 --> 16:47.886 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, can you
characterize the meeting

16:47.886 --> 16:50.176 align:middle
in terms of, you said,
strongly differing views?

16:50.286 --> 16:53.136 align:middle
Was this a close call, this cut?

16:53.136 --> 16:55.046 align:middle
Or was it a close call
maybe the other way,

16:55.046 --> 16:57.166 align:middle
because you had dissents
on both sides?

16:57.166 --> 16:57.936 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

16:58.726 --> 16:59.706 align:middle
So I was referring

16:59.706 --> 17:03.986 align:middle
to the discussion about-to the
extent it related to December.

17:04.316 --> 17:08.476 align:middle
You saw we had two dissents,
one for 50 and one for no cut.

17:08.596 --> 17:12.476 align:middle
So that was a strong, solid
vote in favor of this cut.

17:13.046 --> 17:19.226 align:middle
The strongly differing views
were really about the future,

17:19.226 --> 17:20.126 align:middle
what does that look like?

17:20.676 --> 17:23.866 align:middle
And I think people are
saying-they're noticing stronger

17:23.866 --> 17:24.746 align:middle
economic activity.

17:25.276 --> 17:27.666 align:middle
Forecasters generally, broadly,

17:27.666 --> 17:30.586 align:middle
have raised their economic
growth forecast for this year

17:30.586 --> 17:32.466 align:middle
and next year, in some
cases quite materially.

17:32.766 --> 17:37.516 align:middle
In the meantime, we see a labor
market that's kind of-I don't

17:37.516 --> 17:42.356 align:middle
want to say "stable," but
it's not clearly in motion,

17:42.386 --> 17:45.006 align:middle
it's not clearly declining
quickly in any case.

17:45.006 --> 17:47.046 align:middle
It may be just continuing
to gradually cool.

17:47.676 --> 17:49.696 align:middle
And, again, people have
different-they have different

17:49.696 --> 17:51.766 align:middle
forecasts and expectations
about the economy

17:51.766 --> 17:54.856 align:middle
and different risk tolerances.

17:54.856 --> 17:58.176 align:middle
And so there's-you read the
SEP, you read the speeches,

17:58.586 --> 18:00.366 align:middle
you know there are differing
views on the Committee

18:00.396 --> 18:02.566 align:middle
and to the point where
I said what I said.

18:02.766 --> 18:03.276 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

18:03.276 --> 18:04.986 align:middle
Just a follow-up on
the balance sheet.

18:06.546 --> 18:10.386 align:middle
If you stop it-the runoff-now,
does that mean you have

18:10.446 --> 18:14.506 align:middle
to go back to actually adding
assets sometime next year

18:14.506 --> 18:17.766 align:middle
so that the balance sheet
doesn't shrink as a percent

18:17.766 --> 18:19.546 align:middle
of GDP and become a
tightening factor?

18:19.906 --> 18:20.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:20.186 --> 18:20.836 align:middle
So you're right.

18:21.106 --> 18:23.956 align:middle
The place we'll be on
December 1 is that the size

18:23.956 --> 18:25.476 align:middle
of the balance sheet is frozen,

18:25.976 --> 18:28.276 align:middle
and as mortgage-backed
securities mature,

18:28.376 --> 18:30.196 align:middle
we'll reinvest those
in Treasury bills,

18:30.746 --> 18:34.426 align:middle
which will foster both a
more Treasury balance sheet

18:34.426 --> 18:35.746 align:middle
and also a shorter duration.

18:35.746 --> 18:38.506 align:middle
So that's-in the meantime,
if you freeze the size

18:38.506 --> 18:41.416 align:middle
of the balance sheet, the
nonreserve liabilities-currency,

18:41.416 --> 18:43.756 align:middle
for example-they're going to
continue to grow organically.

18:44.116 --> 18:45.906 align:middle
And, because the size of
the balance sheet is frozen,

18:46.166 --> 18:48.666 align:middle
you have further
shrinkage in reserves.

18:48.666 --> 18:49.566 align:middle
And reserves is the thing

18:49.566 --> 18:52.086 align:middle
that we're-that we're
managing that has to be ample.

18:52.536 --> 18:54.806 align:middle
So that'll happen for a time,

18:54.806 --> 18:56.326 align:middle
but not a tremendously
long time.

18:56.326 --> 18:57.506 align:middle
We don't know exactly how long.

18:57.846 --> 19:01.056 align:middle
But at a certain point, you'll
want to start-you'll want

19:01.056 --> 19:04.436 align:middle
to start reserves to start
gradually growing to keep

19:04.436 --> 19:06.746 align:middle
up with the size of
the banking system

19:06.746 --> 19:07.896 align:middle
and the size of the economy.

19:07.896 --> 19:10.726 align:middle
So we'll be adding
reserves at a certain point,

19:11.316 --> 19:12.876 align:middle
and that's the last point.

19:12.876 --> 19:18.006 align:middle
Even then we'll be-we didn't
make decisions about this today,

19:18.216 --> 19:20.876 align:middle
but we did talk today
about the composition

19:20.876 --> 19:21.406 align:middle
of the balance sheet.

19:21.406 --> 19:26.866 align:middle
And there's a desire that the
balance sheet be-right now it's

19:26.866 --> 19:30.466 align:middle
got a lot more duration than
the outstanding universe

19:30.466 --> 19:33.006 align:middle
of Treasury securities, and
we want to move to a place

19:33.006 --> 19:34.396 align:middle
where we're closer
to that duration.

19:34.396 --> 19:35.396 align:middle
That'll take some time.

19:35.396 --> 19:37.936 align:middle
We haven't made a decision
about the ultimate endpoint,

19:38.356 --> 19:40.976 align:middle
but we all agree that we want
to move more in the direction

19:41.016 --> 19:44.126 align:middle
of a balance sheet that more
closely reflects the outstanding

19:44.556 --> 19:48.106 align:middle
Treasuries, and that means a
shorter-duration balance sheet.

19:48.186 --> 19:51.306 align:middle
Now, this is something that's
going to be-take a long time

19:51.306 --> 19:53.016 align:middle
and move very, very gradually,

19:53.016 --> 19:55.316 align:middle
and I don't think you'll
notice it in market conditions.

19:55.766 --> 19:59.426 align:middle
But that's the direction
of things.

19:59.526 --> 20:01.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:02.746 --> 20:03.966 align:middle
Jonnelle. JONNELLE MARTE.

20:04.366 --> 20:05.766 align:middle
Jonnelle Marte with Bloomberg.

20:06.126 --> 20:09.816 align:middle
How are officials interpreting
the latest CPI report?

20:10.366 --> 20:13.366 align:middle
So some components came
in lower than expected,

20:13.366 --> 20:16.296 align:middle
but core inflation was
still at 3 percent.

20:16.966 --> 20:19.866 align:middle
So at this moment
are-what are you learning

20:19.866 --> 20:22.786 align:middle
about the drivers and,
also, do you believe

20:22.786 --> 20:25.126 align:middle
that the risks are greater
that the Fed makes a mistake

20:25.126 --> 20:26.746 align:middle
on employment or inflation?

20:26.746 --> 20:28.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:28.406 --> 20:33.646 align:middle
So, okay, so the September
CPI report, we didn't get PPI

20:33.646 --> 20:36.526 align:middle
after that, which is-which
is important for translation

20:36.526 --> 20:38.766 align:middle
into what we look at,
which is PCE inflation.

20:39.176 --> 20:41.316 align:middle
But we can still make a
pretty good assessment

20:41.316 --> 20:42.156 align:middle
of what that will be.

20:42.766 --> 20:45.726 align:middle
When we get PPI, there
might be some adjustments.

20:45.766 --> 20:50.596 align:middle
But so directionally, it was
a little softer than expected,

20:50.796 --> 20:52.926 align:middle
and we always break it down
into the three components.

20:52.926 --> 20:57.486 align:middle
So, basically, you've seen
goods prices increasing,

20:57.486 --> 20:59.106 align:middle
and that's really
due to tariffs,

20:59.566 --> 21:03.436 align:middle
and that's due-that's compared
to a longer-run trend of very,

21:03.436 --> 21:05.296 align:middle
very mild deflation in goods.

21:05.636 --> 21:07.326 align:middle
So that's moving inflation up.

21:07.646 --> 21:08.636 align:middle
On the other side of that,

21:09.136 --> 21:12.936 align:middle
good news-housing services
inflation has been coming down

21:12.936 --> 21:14.366 align:middle
and is expected to
continue to come down.

21:14.366 --> 21:17.246 align:middle
So if you remember, a year-a
couple years ago, that's the one

21:17.246 --> 21:20.386 align:middle
that just, we kept
expecting it to do that.

21:20.386 --> 21:22.436 align:middle
Now it's doing-it's been
doing that for some time,

21:22.436 --> 21:23.586 align:middle
and we expect to continue that.

21:24.426 --> 21:27.386 align:middle
That leaves-the biggest
category is services other

21:27.386 --> 21:28.476 align:middle
than housing services.

21:28.856 --> 21:30.956 align:middle
And that's kind of
been moving sideways

21:31.946 --> 21:34.806 align:middle
over the last few months,
but a significant part

21:34.806 --> 21:38.096 align:middle
of that is nonmarket services.

21:38.096 --> 21:40.846 align:middle
We don't take a lot of
signal about the tightness

21:40.846 --> 21:42.116 align:middle
of the economy from that.

21:42.296 --> 21:45.066 align:middle
So if you add all that up,
a couple of things to say.

21:45.066 --> 21:49.796 align:middle
One is that inflation away from
tariffs is actually not so far

21:49.796 --> 21:50.946 align:middle
from our 2 percent goal.

21:51.246 --> 21:53.786 align:middle
We estimate-people have
different estimates of what

21:53.786 --> 21:58.646 align:middle
that is, but it might
be five- or six-tenths,

21:58.706 --> 22:04.906 align:middle
and so if it's 2.8, then core
PCE, not including tariffs,

22:05.476 --> 22:08.906 align:middle
might be 2.3 or 2.4, in that
range, something like that.

22:08.906 --> 22:11.236 align:middle
So that's not so
far from your goal.

22:11.756 --> 22:15.196 align:middle
So that-we look at
that, and the thing

22:15.196 --> 22:18.826 align:middle
about tariff inflation is the
base case is that it will come,

22:19.466 --> 22:22.436 align:middle
and it probably will
increase further.

22:23.256 --> 22:25.656 align:middle
But it is that it will
be a one-time increase,

22:25.786 --> 22:30.376 align:middle
and we've been very focused for
all of this year at making sure

22:30.376 --> 22:32.436 align:middle
that that's the case
and thinking carefully

22:32.436 --> 22:35.026 align:middle
about what are the-what
are the pathways

22:35.026 --> 22:36.646 align:middle
through which it could
become something else,

22:36.786 --> 22:37.906 align:middle
troublesome inflation.

22:37.906 --> 22:40.976 align:middle
One of those would be a
really tight labor market.

22:41.066 --> 22:41.746 align:middle
We don't see that.

22:41.986 --> 22:44.236 align:middle
Another could be inflation
expectations moving.

22:44.236 --> 22:44.966 align:middle
We don't see that.

22:45.416 --> 22:48.746 align:middle
So I think-we're watching
this very carefully.

22:48.746 --> 22:51.746 align:middle
I think it's-it's not the
case that we're just assuming

22:52.136 --> 22:53.736 align:middle
that it's going to be
a one-time inflation.

22:53.736 --> 22:56.456 align:middle
We understand fully that this
is a risk we have to monitor

22:56.776 --> 22:57.686 align:middle
and ultimately manage.

22:58.296 --> 22:59.086 align:middle
JONNELLE MARTE.

22:59.306 --> 23:01.796 align:middle
With a stubborn services
inflation, what are some

23:01.796 --> 23:04.396 align:middle
of the things that the Fed
could do to address that,

23:04.596 --> 23:07.846 align:middle
and especially when we're
seeing potentially labor

23:07.946 --> 23:09.206 align:middle
supply challenges?

23:09.506 --> 23:09.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:09.856 --> 23:11.486 align:middle
Sorry, say that first-
JONNELLE MARTE.

23:11.536 --> 23:13.446 align:middle
Stubborn services
inflation- CHAIR POWELL.

23:13.446 --> 23:14.556 align:middle
Oh, stubborn services inflation.

23:15.236 --> 23:18.076 align:middle
Well, again, the part
of services inflation

23:18.076 --> 23:22.196 align:middle
that is-that isn't coming
down as we would like it

23:22.196 --> 23:27.566 align:middle
to is the nonmarket part
of nonhousing services.

23:28.246 --> 23:30.966 align:middle
And, overall, that's
just something

23:31.196 --> 23:32.736 align:middle
that we expect will come down.

23:32.736 --> 23:35.596 align:middle
The nonhousing-the nonmarket
part of it should come down.

23:35.596 --> 23:40.266 align:middle
It largely reflects higher stock
prices and financial services

23:40.346 --> 23:43.336 align:middle
that are imputed, rather
than actually paid,

23:43.336 --> 23:45.096 align:middle
is a big part of that.

23:45.336 --> 23:48.896 align:middle
But also, just-we think policy
is still modestly restrictive

23:49.056 --> 23:49.756 align:middle
in my telling.

23:49.756 --> 23:51.746 align:middle
So that's the kind of
thing that should lead

23:51.746 --> 23:53.296 align:middle
to a gradually cooling economy.

23:53.296 --> 23:55.536 align:middle
That's one of the reasons you
see a gradually cooling labor

23:55.536 --> 24:02.246 align:middle
market, [it] is because Fed
policy is modestly restrictive.

24:02.246 --> 24:03.986 align:middle
So that should also
help get that.

24:04.316 --> 24:06.956 align:middle
I want to say, though,
we're absolutely committed

24:06.956 --> 24:08.816 align:middle
to returning inflation
to 2 percent.

24:09.246 --> 24:12.216 align:middle
If you look at longer-term
surveys or market pricing,

24:12.476 --> 24:15.256 align:middle
you will see that that's
a credible commitment,

24:15.416 --> 24:18.936 align:middle
and there should be no question
that that's where we're going.

24:20.126 --> 24:21.076 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:21.426 --> 24:23.496 align:middle
Chris. CHRIS RUGABER.

24:24.466 --> 24:25.106 align:middle
Great, thank you.

24:25.106 --> 24:26.706 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

24:27.256 --> 24:30.966 align:middle
So there's a big investment boom
in AI infrastructure right now,

24:31.026 --> 24:34.046 align:middle
as you know, and
wondering if the existence

24:34.376 --> 24:37.246 align:middle
of such a boom would
indicate that rates are not

24:37.246 --> 24:40.036 align:middle
that restrictive after all,
and could further rate cuts

24:40.036 --> 24:42.246 align:middle
at this point perhaps
fuel an excess level

24:42.246 --> 24:45.466 align:middle
of investment there
or market bubbles?

24:46.086 --> 24:47.426 align:middle
How is the Fed thinking
about that?

24:47.696 --> 24:48.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:49.396 --> 24:53.196 align:middle
So I don't-I don't think
that the-you're right,

24:54.036 --> 24:57.756 align:middle
there's a-there's a lot of
data centers being built

24:58.196 --> 24:59.996 align:middle
and other investments being made

25:00.126 --> 25:02.636 align:middle
around the country
and around the world.

25:03.256 --> 25:09.586 align:middle
And big U.S. companies are just
investing a lot of resources

25:09.696 --> 25:13.246 align:middle
in thinking about how
AI, which will be based

25:13.246 --> 25:15.916 align:middle
on those data centers,
run through data centers,

25:16.046 --> 25:17.236 align:middle
is going to affect
their businesses.

25:17.236 --> 25:18.296 align:middle
So it's a big deal.

25:18.946 --> 25:23.506 align:middle
I don't think that the
spending that happens

25:23.506 --> 25:24.816 align:middle
to build data centers all

25:24.816 --> 25:26.906 align:middle
over the country is
especially interest sensitive.

25:27.436 --> 25:31.116 align:middle
It's based on longer-run,
it's-longer-run assessments

25:31.156 --> 25:34.546 align:middle
that this is an area where
there's going to be a lot

25:34.546 --> 25:37.006 align:middle
of investment and that's going
to drive higher productivity

25:37.006 --> 25:37.736 align:middle
and that sort of things.

25:37.996 --> 25:40.466 align:middle
I don't know how those
investments will work out,

25:40.466 --> 25:43.026 align:middle
but I don't think they're
particularly interest sensitive

25:43.066 --> 25:44.356 align:middle
compared to some of
the other sectors.

25:44.636 --> 25:45.496 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

25:45.496 --> 25:46.466 align:middle
And then just a quick follow-up.

25:46.466 --> 25:48.596 align:middle
You mentioned that you do
have data that you're looking

25:48.596 --> 25:51.426 align:middle
at for inflation and growth in
the absence of government data.

25:51.826 --> 25:54.946 align:middle
Could you give us a sense-and
I think we know a lot

25:54.946 --> 25:57.736 align:middle
about the jobs data that's out
there-can you give us a sense

25:57.736 --> 25:59.506 align:middle
of what you're looking
at to track inflation

25:59.506 --> 26:00.956 align:middle
in the absence of
government data?

26:01.346 --> 26:01.616 align:middle
Thank you.

26:01.786 --> 26:02.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:02.086 --> 26:03.046 align:middle
So it's a lot of things.

26:03.046 --> 26:05.316 align:middle
And it doesn't replace
government data,

26:05.316 --> 26:09.176 align:middle
but you know all of these,
it's-I'll just mention some

26:09.176 --> 26:13.756 align:middle
of the many, many names:
PriceStats, Adobe, and others.

26:14.736 --> 26:19.266 align:middle
And for wage inflation,
there's ADP data.

26:19.916 --> 26:22.596 align:middle
On spending-you're going
to ask about spending

26:22.596 --> 26:25.006 align:middle
at some point-there are lots of
other things that we look at.

26:25.006 --> 26:27.226 align:middle
But it's, again,
it's, it's many,

26:27.756 --> 26:30.916 align:middle
many different sources and,
again, including what we get

26:30.916 --> 26:35.006 align:middle
out of the Beige Book,
which will be sort of-come

26:35.006 --> 26:36.316 align:middle
out mid-cycle as always.

26:37.056 --> 26:40.596 align:middle
And it doesn't replace
the government data,

26:40.596 --> 26:41.686 align:middle
but it gives us a picture.

26:41.966 --> 26:44.956 align:middle
Again, I think if something
material were happening,

26:44.956 --> 26:46.086 align:middle
if there were material
developments,

26:46.086 --> 26:47.216 align:middle
I think we would pick that up.

26:47.646 --> 26:49.216 align:middle
I don't think we'll be able

26:49.216 --> 26:50.906 align:middle
to have a very granular
understanding

26:50.906 --> 26:54.956 align:middle
of the economy while this-while
this data is not available.

26:55.346 --> 26:56.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:56.156 --> 27:01.046 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:01.716 --> 27:03.286 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

27:03.286 --> 27:03.656 align:middle
Thank you.

27:03.956 --> 27:05.646 align:middle
I just wanted you to
elaborate a little bit

27:05.646 --> 27:06.806 align:middle
on what you said a moment ago

27:06.806 --> 27:10.336 align:middle
about the lack-the continued
shutdown making it more

27:10.336 --> 27:12.146 align:middle
difficult to make
a move in December,

27:12.646 --> 27:15.416 align:middle
and that may make
you more cautious.

27:15.416 --> 27:18.506 align:middle
To the degree you are
relying on private data

27:18.506 --> 27:21.596 align:middle
that isn't the gold standard
or that you're relying

27:21.596 --> 27:24.106 align:middle
on your own surveys or the
Beige Book, do you worry

27:24.106 --> 27:25.326 align:middle
at some point you're
going to have

27:25.326 --> 27:27.006 align:middle
to start making policy
by anecdote?

27:28.216 --> 27:28.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:29.026 --> 27:30.546 align:middle
This is a temporary
state of affairs.

27:30.726 --> 27:32.616 align:middle
And we're going to do our jobs,

27:33.096 --> 27:36.036 align:middle
we're going to collect every
scrap of data we can find,

27:36.036 --> 27:38.166 align:middle
evaluate it, and think
carefully about it.

27:38.226 --> 27:42.296 align:middle
And that's our jobs-that's
what we're going to do.

27:42.536 --> 27:46.036 align:middle
If you ask me, "Could it
affect the December meeting?",

27:46.396 --> 27:47.896 align:middle
I'm not saying it's
going to, but, yeah,

27:47.896 --> 27:51.206 align:middle
you could imagine that-what
do you do if-what do you do

27:51.206 --> 27:52.446 align:middle
if you're driving in the fog?

27:52.446 --> 27:53.226 align:middle
You slow down.

27:53.226 --> 27:55.436 align:middle
So that could or could not.

27:55.436 --> 27:57.476 align:middle
I don't know how that's
going to play into things.

27:58.056 --> 28:01.136 align:middle
We may get-the data
may come back.

28:01.136 --> 28:03.926 align:middle
But there's a possibility
that it would make sense

28:03.926 --> 28:05.396 align:middle
to be more cautious
about moving.

28:05.396 --> 28:07.216 align:middle
I'm not-I'm not committing
to that,

28:07.216 --> 28:09.366 align:middle
I'm just saying it's certainly a
possibility that you would say,

28:10.406 --> 28:13.246 align:middle
"We really can't see,
so let's slow down."

28:13.866 --> 28:14.336 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

28:14.336 --> 28:17.956 align:middle
As a follow-up, in the
debate on this meeting,

28:17.956 --> 28:21.446 align:middle
we saw recently some pretty
big layoff announcements coming

28:21.506 --> 28:24.716 align:middle
from Amazon and others-wondering
if that figured

28:24.716 --> 28:27.406 align:middle
into the discussion at all that
you're starting to see the turn,

28:27.406 --> 28:29.816 align:middle
this tension between growth
and employment starting

28:29.816 --> 28:33.646 align:middle
to be resolved to the
detriment of employment.

28:33.646 --> 28:37.066 align:middle
And, secondly, some of the
stresses starting to appear

28:37.066 --> 28:39.876 align:middle
in the bottom spur of
the K, as they call it,

28:39.876 --> 28:42.566 align:middle
household health
premiums that are going

28:42.566 --> 28:45.766 align:middle
to be-possibly be going up quite
substantially, things like that.

28:45.766 --> 28:47.496 align:middle
Has that started
to become a factor

28:47.496 --> 28:48.786 align:middle
in your policy discussion?

28:48.786 --> 28:49.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:49.206 --> 28:50.796 align:middle
So those are-those
are both things

28:50.886 --> 28:54.006 align:middle
that we're watching very,
very, very carefully.

28:54.006 --> 28:56.036 align:middle
To start with the layoffs,

28:56.036 --> 28:59.376 align:middle
you're right-you see
a significant number

28:59.376 --> 29:02.166 align:middle
of companies either announcing
that they are not going

29:02.166 --> 29:04.546 align:middle
to be doing much hiring
or actually doing layoffs,

29:05.196 --> 29:08.346 align:middle
and much of the time
they're talking

29:08.346 --> 29:09.716 align:middle
about AI and what it can do.

29:10.186 --> 29:12.566 align:middle
So we're watching
that very carefully.

29:13.226 --> 29:17.926 align:middle
And, yes, it could
absolutely have implications

29:17.926 --> 29:18.856 align:middle
for job creation.

29:19.106 --> 29:23.616 align:middle
We don't really see it in
the initial claims data yet.

29:24.186 --> 29:27.296 align:middle
Now, it's not a surprise that we
don't-it takes some time for it

29:27.296 --> 29:29.896 align:middle
to get in there-but we're
watching that really carefully.

29:30.486 --> 29:35.786 align:middle
But, again, don't see it yet in
the-in the initial claims data.

29:35.786 --> 29:38.416 align:middle
On the K-shaped economy thing,

29:38.416 --> 29:41.056 align:middle
I would say the same
thing, or similar thing.

29:41.576 --> 29:47.276 align:middle
We are-if you listen to the
earnings calls or the reports

29:47.336 --> 29:51.656 align:middle
of big, public, consumer-facing
companies, many,

29:51.656 --> 29:52.986 align:middle
many of them are saying

29:53.056 --> 29:57.696 align:middle
that there's a bifurcated
economy there and that consumers

29:57.696 --> 30:01.126 align:middle
at the lower end are struggling
and buying less and shifting

30:01.126 --> 30:04.376 align:middle
to lower-cost products, but that
at the top, people are spending

30:04.596 --> 30:08.506 align:middle
at the higher income and
wealth, and they're-so much,

30:08.576 --> 30:11.746 align:middle
much anecdotal data on that.

30:11.926 --> 30:14.306 align:middle
And so we think there's
something there-so.

30:15.826 --> 30:17.006 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:18.246 --> 30:20.566 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

30:20.716 --> 30:21.186 align:middle
Thank you.

30:21.186 --> 30:23.036 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

30:23.036 --> 30:26.876 align:middle
So Mr. Chairman, I want
to take another crack

30:26.996 --> 30:28.346 align:middle
at the further reduction

30:28.346 --> 30:29.956 align:middle
in rates is not a
foregone conclusion

30:29.956 --> 30:31.606 align:middle
in December-you said
"far from it."

30:31.606 --> 30:35.776 align:middle
So if a cut might not be
on the table for December

30:36.006 --> 30:38.746 align:middle
because of lack of data, what
is the other concerns then

30:38.746 --> 30:39.386 align:middle
stem from?

30:40.466 --> 30:42.176 align:middle
So if it's not lack of data

30:42.566 --> 30:45.786 align:middle
as the reason December is
not a foregone conclusion,

30:46.196 --> 30:48.256 align:middle
what other things could
be the concern then?

30:48.336 --> 30:48.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:48.736 --> 30:51.206 align:middle
Well, perspectives of
people on the Committee

30:51.726 --> 30:56.306 align:middle
that we've now moved 150
basis points and that we're

30:56.306 --> 31:00.866 align:middle
down into-you're into that
range between 3 and 4,

31:00.866 --> 31:04.636 align:middle
where most estimates
of-many estimates

31:04.746 --> 31:07.596 align:middle
of the neutral rate live in
that 3 to 4 percent area.

31:07.596 --> 31:08.226 align:middle
You're there now.

31:08.226 --> 31:11.636 align:middle
You're above the median
number for the Committee.

31:11.636 --> 31:13.066 align:middle
But I think there are
people on the Committee

31:13.066 --> 31:15.586 align:middle
who have higher estimates
of the neutral rate,

31:15.586 --> 31:17.556 align:middle
and that's-you can
argue these positions

31:17.556 --> 31:20.206 align:middle
since it can't be directly
observed, the neutral rate.

31:20.206 --> 31:26.246 align:middle
So I think for some
part of the Committee,

31:26.896 --> 31:30.186 align:middle
it's time to maybe
take a step back

31:31.026 --> 31:34.936 align:middle
and see whether there
really are downside risks

31:34.936 --> 31:37.446 align:middle
to the labor market, or
see whether, in fact,

31:37.626 --> 31:40.256 align:middle
the growth-that the
stronger growth

31:40.256 --> 31:42.086 align:middle
that we're seeing is real.

31:42.786 --> 31:45.856 align:middle
Ordinarily, the labor
market is a better indicator

31:45.856 --> 31:48.906 align:middle
of the momentum of the
economy than the spending data.

31:49.336 --> 31:50.216 align:middle
That's the allure.

31:50.796 --> 31:54.016 align:middle
In this case, that gives
a more downbeat read.

31:55.116 --> 31:59.646 align:middle
So people just have-again,
we've cut 50 more basis points

32:00.026 --> 32:04.996 align:middle
in the last two meetings and
there was a sense, from some,

32:05.236 --> 32:06.716 align:middle
"let's pause here"
kind of thing,

32:07.046 --> 32:09.686 align:middle
and a sense from others
wanting to go ahead.

32:09.756 --> 32:14.246 align:middle
But that's why I say differing
views-strongly differing views.

32:14.246 --> 32:14.696 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

32:14.696 --> 32:15.826 align:middle
So on that division, then,

32:15.826 --> 32:17.566 align:middle
you're talking about
going forward.

32:17.816 --> 32:20.086 align:middle
So what's more important
in this division?

32:20.086 --> 32:21.276 align:middle
Is it inflation risks?

32:21.276 --> 32:22.496 align:middle
Is it employment risks?

32:22.496 --> 32:26.186 align:middle
Or is there a deeper philosophy
division among the Board?

32:26.646 --> 32:26.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:26.946 --> 32:30.706 align:middle
I-look, everybody on the
Committee is deeply committed

32:31.346 --> 32:34.026 align:middle
to doing the right thing
to achieve our goals:

32:34.026 --> 32:35.906 align:middle
maximum employment
and stable prices.

32:36.426 --> 32:37.956 align:middle
You have differences
on how to do that.

32:38.096 --> 32:40.786 align:middle
And as I mentioned, some of
that is different forecasts,

32:40.786 --> 32:44.886 align:middle
but a lot of it is also
different risk aversions

32:44.886 --> 32:47.906 align:middle
to the two different variables,
which is common through,

32:48.786 --> 32:50.586 align:middle
through all federal reserves,

32:50.686 --> 32:54.266 align:middle
they're-people just have
different risk tolerances,

32:54.266 --> 32:54.696 align:middle
let's say.

32:54.696 --> 32:57.186 align:middle
So that leads you to
people with disparate views.

32:57.186 --> 32:59.636 align:middle
You will know that from the
speeches you've been listening

32:59.636 --> 33:00.686 align:middle
to from my colleagues.

33:01.316 --> 33:04.256 align:middle
And so we're at a place
now where we have, in fact,

33:04.256 --> 33:09.306 align:middle
cut two more times, and now
we're 150 basis points closer

33:09.306 --> 33:11.716 align:middle
to neutral, wherever that may
be, than we were a year ago.

33:12.276 --> 33:15.806 align:middle
And so there's a growing
chorus now of feeling

33:15.806 --> 33:17.546 align:middle
like maybe this is
where we should

33:17.546 --> 33:19.916 align:middle
at least wait a cycle,
something like that.

33:20.006 --> 33:20.656 align:middle
That's what it is.

33:20.656 --> 33:21.756 align:middle
It's just what you
think it would be.

33:22.206 --> 33:26.366 align:middle
It's-and, again, you've seen
it in the September Summary

33:26.366 --> 33:27.316 align:middle
of Economic Projections.

33:27.316 --> 33:31.416 align:middle
You've seen this in the public
remarks of FOMC participants.

33:31.416 --> 33:34.396 align:middle
And now I'm telling you that's
what-that's what you can expect

33:34.396 --> 33:35.066 align:middle
in the minutes.

33:35.626 --> 33:37.766 align:middle
And I'm just telling you that's
what happened in the meeting.

33:39.466 --> 33:39.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:40.316 --> 33:40.556 align:middle
Elizabeth.

33:40.556 --> 33:41.066 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

33:41.936 --> 33:42.366 align:middle
Thank you so much.

33:42.456 --> 33:45.026 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

33:45.026 --> 33:48.816 align:middle
What is your explanation for
why the job market is weakening

33:48.816 --> 33:51.106 align:middle
right now, and what
will this rate cut do

33:51.166 --> 33:52.576 align:middle
to improve the job market?

33:53.586 --> 33:54.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:54.356 --> 33:58.296 align:middle
So the-I think there are two
things affecting the job market,

33:58.296 --> 34:02.506 align:middle
and one of them is just
a dramatic reduction

34:02.506 --> 34:04.366 align:middle
in the supply of new workers.

34:04.866 --> 34:09.756 align:middle
So and that's two things-that's
declining labor force

34:09.756 --> 34:12.416 align:middle
participation, which
is a cyclical thing,

34:12.416 --> 34:15.586 align:middle
and then there's declining
immigration-which is just a big

34:15.586 --> 34:18.356 align:middle
policy change that actually
began in the last Administration

34:18.356 --> 34:19.646 align:middle
and has been accelerated now.

34:20.216 --> 34:24.486 align:middle
So a big part of the whole
story is that supply-side story.

34:24.726 --> 34:27.446 align:middle
Okay. In addition, labor
demand has declined,

34:27.446 --> 34:30.436 align:middle
and so the labor-the
unemployment rate has gone down,

34:30.436 --> 34:32.196 align:middle
meaning that demand
for workers has gone

34:32.196 --> 34:34.226 align:middle
down a little more than supply.

34:34.796 --> 34:37.526 align:middle
So that's what's going on,

34:38.006 --> 34:40.386 align:middle
and-but it is mostly
a supply function.

34:40.646 --> 34:44.686 align:middle
It's mostly a function of
the change in supply, I think

34:44.926 --> 34:46.166 align:middle
and many people think.

34:46.556 --> 34:50.586 align:middle
So the question then is, what
does-what does our tool do,

34:50.586 --> 34:51.756 align:middle
which supports demand?

34:52.346 --> 34:56.666 align:middle
And so-and I would just say,
when you're in a situation

34:57.386 --> 35:03.946 align:middle
where job creation, if you
adjust for likely overcounting

35:04.216 --> 35:07.756 align:middle
in the way that BLS does its
work, is pretty close to zero.

35:08.486 --> 35:12.566 align:middle
So maximum employment doesn't,
on a sustainable basis,

35:12.606 --> 35:15.686 align:middle
doesn't-if you're making,
creating zero jobs,

35:16.266 --> 35:19.306 align:middle
if it's in equilibrium, if
it's in balance, it's a pretty,

35:19.786 --> 35:22.286 align:middle
as I said before,
pretty curious balance.

35:22.626 --> 35:26.326 align:middle
So I thought, and many of my
colleagues thought-in fact,

35:26.586 --> 35:29.246 align:middle
you've seen the last two
meetings that it was appropriate

35:29.246 --> 35:33.686 align:middle
for us to react by supporting
demand with our rates.

35:33.686 --> 35:36.226 align:middle
And we've done that-we've
reduced

35:36.226 --> 35:37.716 align:middle
so that rates are looser.

35:37.776 --> 35:40.246 align:middle
I wouldn't say that they're
accommodative right now,

35:40.246 --> 35:42.706 align:middle
but they're meaningfully
less tight than they were.

35:43.176 --> 35:45.506 align:middle
And that should help so that

35:45.506 --> 35:47.726 align:middle
at least the labor
market doesn't get worse.

35:48.216 --> 35:51.356 align:middle
It's a complicated situation,
and some people argue

35:51.526 --> 35:54.256 align:middle
that this is supply and we
really can't affect it much

35:54.256 --> 35:57.896 align:middle
with our tools, but
others argue, as I do,

35:57.896 --> 35:59.776 align:middle
that there is an
effect from demand,

35:59.776 --> 36:02.416 align:middle
and that we should use our tools
to support the labor market

36:02.416 --> 36:03.336 align:middle
when we see this happening.

36:03.976 --> 36:04.566 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

36:04.596 --> 36:06.826 align:middle
You also talked about
tariffs causing a one-time

36:06.826 --> 36:07.686 align:middle
price increase.

36:07.686 --> 36:09.916 align:middle
Should American households,
consumers expect

36:10.066 --> 36:12.596 align:middle
that inflation will
continue to go up this year

36:12.596 --> 36:13.156 align:middle
because of those tariffs?

36:13.156 --> 36:14.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:14.596 --> 36:17.676 align:middle
So the basic expectation is

36:17.676 --> 36:20.706 align:middle
that there will be some
additional increased inflation

36:20.706 --> 36:24.566 align:middle
because it takes a while for
tariffs to work their way

36:24.646 --> 36:28.926 align:middle
through the production chain
and finally get to consumers.

36:29.306 --> 36:31.926 align:middle
And we see this now from
the tariffs that were put

36:31.926 --> 36:35.126 align:middle
in place now many months
ago-we see those effects.

36:35.166 --> 36:38.256 align:middle
But if you put tariffs in
effect-and they've been coming

36:38.256 --> 36:42.066 align:middle
into effect consistently
in February, March, April,

36:42.066 --> 36:45.296 align:middle
May-and that's all happening,
so that'll continue to happen

36:45.296 --> 36:47.116 align:middle
for some time, probably
into the spring.

36:47.296 --> 36:48.906 align:middle
These are not big
increases, though.

36:49.266 --> 36:51.136 align:middle
These are a tenth
or so on inflation.

36:51.486 --> 36:52.476 align:middle
They may be big increases

36:52.476 --> 36:55.556 align:middle
on a particular product that's
been tariffed, but, overall,

36:55.556 --> 36:56.636 align:middle
these are fairly modest.

36:56.636 --> 36:58.556 align:middle
I think some projections-we're

36:58.556 --> 37:01.126 align:middle
at 2.8 percent inflation-you
might get two

37:01.126 --> 37:03.256 align:middle
or three more tenths, or
four more tenths maybe.

37:03.806 --> 37:07.016 align:middle
But then, as all
the tariffs are in,

37:07.786 --> 37:09.556 align:middle
they stop generating inflation.

37:09.596 --> 37:11.316 align:middle
You've had a one-time
price increase.

37:11.316 --> 37:13.516 align:middle
As long as you're not-at
least this is the theory,

37:13.516 --> 37:15.056 align:middle
this is how we believe and hope

37:15.616 --> 37:19.966 align:middle
that it will work out-once the
last tariff is put on something,

37:20.706 --> 37:23.986 align:middle
at that point it becomes
a higher price level,

37:23.986 --> 37:27.506 align:middle
but it stops going up, if you
will, prices stop going up,

37:27.506 --> 37:28.636 align:middle
they'll just be at that level.

37:28.636 --> 37:31.296 align:middle
And then measured
inflation will come back

37:31.296 --> 37:32.856 align:middle
down to non-tariff inflation.

37:32.856 --> 37:35.676 align:middle
And as I mentioned,
non-tariff inflation is not

37:35.676 --> 37:36.996 align:middle
so far from 2 percent now.

37:37.226 --> 37:40.926 align:middle
Now, consumers are not
interested in that story.

37:41.186 --> 37:42.836 align:middle
Their prices are higher.

37:43.216 --> 37:45.906 align:middle
More than that, the
reason they're so unhappy

37:45.906 --> 37:49.026 align:middle
about inflation is the inflation
that we had in 2021, '22,

37:49.026 --> 37:53.596 align:middle
'23-because you can say that
prices aren't going up as much,

37:53.646 --> 37:56.356 align:middle
but that doesn't mean people
aren't feeling those higher

37:56.356 --> 38:00.186 align:middle
prices from the inflation we
had two or three years ago.

38:00.186 --> 38:02.216 align:middle
They are. And that's,
I think, a large part

38:02.216 --> 38:04.106 align:middle
of why the public-you
sample people,

38:04.646 --> 38:07.676 align:middle
inflation is still very much
making people quite unhappy,

38:08.136 --> 38:13.146 align:middle
even-and it's nice that prices
are not going up as fast

38:13.146 --> 38:15.726 align:middle
as they were, but they're still
much higher than they were.

38:15.856 --> 38:18.636 align:middle
And it'll take some time
for that effect to wear off.

38:18.706 --> 38:22.696 align:middle
As real incomes rise, it
will feel better over time.

38:22.756 --> 38:23.836 align:middle
But that's going to take time.

38:25.346 --> 38:26.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:28.266 --> 38:28.976 align:middle
Mike. MICHAEL MCKEE.

38:28.976 --> 38:30.836 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

38:31.236 --> 38:35.346 align:middle
Do you have any concerns that
equity markets are-or are close

38:35.346 --> 38:37.866 align:middle
to being-overvalued
at this point?

38:38.876 --> 38:39.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:39.536 --> 38:43.436 align:middle
You know, we don't look
at any one asset price

38:43.436 --> 38:44.936 align:middle
and say, "Hey, that's wrong."

38:45.386 --> 38:46.616 align:middle
It's not our job to do that.

38:46.906 --> 38:49.726 align:middle
We look at the overall
financial system,

38:50.276 --> 38:52.146 align:middle
and we ask whether it's stable

38:52.146 --> 38:54.216 align:middle
and whether it could
withstand shocks, right?

38:54.216 --> 38:56.926 align:middle
So banks are well capitalized,

38:57.116 --> 38:59.996 align:middle
while some households
are clearly under stress.

39:00.326 --> 39:03.186 align:middle
In the aggregate, households are

39:03.186 --> 39:05.466 align:middle
in good shape
financially-relatively,

39:06.116 --> 39:09.606 align:middle
relatively manageable
levels of debt.

39:09.716 --> 39:12.866 align:middle
At the lower-income spectrum,
you are seeing rising defaults,

39:12.866 --> 39:14.956 align:middle
particularly in subprime
auto, but, nonetheless,

39:15.246 --> 39:17.516 align:middle
in the aggregate pretty good.

39:18.256 --> 39:21.306 align:middle
And you don't see-so you
don't see too much leverage

39:21.306 --> 39:23.136 align:middle
in the banking system
or the financial system.

39:23.616 --> 39:26.796 align:middle
It's a mixed picture, but it's
not an overly troubling picture.

39:26.916 --> 39:31.746 align:middle
And, again, I'm not-it's
not appropriate-we don't set

39:31.746 --> 39:32.856 align:middle
asset prices.

39:32.856 --> 39:33.706 align:middle
Markets do that.

39:34.386 --> 39:34.806 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

39:34.806 --> 39:36.706 align:middle
Well, are you-you
must be well aware

39:37.306 --> 39:38.996 align:middle
by lowering interest
rates you're contributing

39:39.036 --> 39:42.826 align:middle
to additional asset
price increases.

39:43.176 --> 39:44.586 align:middle
And I wonder how
you balance the idea

39:44.586 --> 39:46.816 align:middle
that lowering rates would
help the labor market

39:46.816 --> 39:49.186 align:middle
with the reality that
it seems more likely

39:49.186 --> 39:52.366 align:middle
to be stimulating
increased investment in AI,

39:52.916 --> 39:55.646 align:middle
which is the rationale
for thousands of job cuts

39:55.726 --> 39:57.606 align:middle
that have been announced
in the last few weeks.

39:58.046 --> 39:58.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:58.726 --> 40:01.846 align:middle
Yeah, I don't-I don't think
interest rates are an important

40:01.846 --> 40:04.776 align:middle
part of the AI-the
data center story.

40:05.316 --> 40:08.256 align:middle
I think-people think
there are great economics

40:08.326 --> 40:11.236 align:middle
in building these data centers,
and they're making a lot

40:11.236 --> 40:12.016 align:middle
of money building them,

40:12.016 --> 40:14.706 align:middle
and I think they have
very high present value

40:14.706 --> 40:15.456 align:middle
and all that sort of thing.

40:15.456 --> 40:22.316 align:middle
It's not really-it's not about
25 basis points here or there.

40:22.316 --> 40:24.346 align:middle
We use our tools to
support the labor market

40:24.466 --> 40:27.196 align:middle
and to create price stability.

40:27.196 --> 40:27.816 align:middle
That's what we do.

40:28.376 --> 40:29.616 align:middle
That's our two jobs, right?

40:29.686 --> 40:34.036 align:middle
So we're here to-by
lowering rates at the margin,

40:34.036 --> 40:37.166 align:middle
that will support demand, and
that will support more hiring.

40:37.636 --> 40:38.546 align:middle
And that's why we do it.

40:38.926 --> 40:43.106 align:middle
Now, no 25 basis point or even
50 basis point hike is going

40:43.106 --> 40:44.236 align:middle
to be a dispositive thing.

40:44.236 --> 40:48.516 align:middle
But, ultimately, lower rates
will support more demand,

40:49.076 --> 40:50.926 align:middle
and that'll support
hiring over time.

40:51.116 --> 40:54.416 align:middle
And, of course, we also have
to be careful about this,

40:54.416 --> 40:56.856 align:middle
which is what we've been
doing because we know

40:56.856 --> 40:59.556 align:middle
where inflation is and we
know-I've told you the story,

40:59.776 --> 41:02.726 align:middle
this complicated story, but
this is the best assessment

41:02.726 --> 41:08.196 align:middle
that we can make and-but
because there's uncertainty

41:08.196 --> 41:10.976 align:middle
around inflation and the
path ahead for inflation,

41:10.976 --> 41:14.286 align:middle
that's why we're going-that's
why the pace we're going has

41:14.286 --> 41:15.026 align:middle
been a careful one.

41:16.856 --> 41:17.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:21.336 --> 41:22.266 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

41:22.266 --> 41:23.956 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
from Politico.

41:23.956 --> 41:26.796 align:middle
On AI, I'm just wondering,
it seems like a lot

41:26.796 --> 41:27.706 align:middle
of the economic growth

41:27.706 --> 41:30.426 align:middle
that we've been seeing is
fed by investments in AI.

41:30.786 --> 41:34.466 align:middle
So how worried are you about
what the sudden contraction

41:34.466 --> 41:36.676 align:middle
in tech investment would
mean for the overall economy?

41:36.676 --> 41:39.506 align:middle
Is there enough strength
in other sectors?

41:39.556 --> 41:42.426 align:middle
And, specifically, are there
any lessons that you take

41:42.426 --> 41:45.306 align:middle
from the 1990s in how you
might approach what's happening

41:45.306 --> 41:45.656 align:middle
right now?

41:45.656 --> 41:46.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:46.836 --> 41:49.576 align:middle
This is-this is different
in the sense

41:49.766 --> 41:51.466 align:middle
that these companies-the
companies that are

41:51.466 --> 41:54.026 align:middle
so highly valued-actually have
earnings and stuff like that.

41:54.026 --> 41:56.506 align:middle
So you go back to the '90s
and the dot-com [period],

41:57.036 --> 41:59.896 align:middle
they were-these were ideas
rather than companies.

42:00.006 --> 42:02.906 align:middle
And we're-so there's
a clear bubble there,

42:03.726 --> 42:06.346 align:middle
whereas the-I won't go
into particular names,

42:06.386 --> 42:09.376 align:middle
but they actually have
earnings, and it looks

42:09.376 --> 42:11.896 align:middle
like they have business
models and profits

42:11.896 --> 42:13.316 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

42:13.316 --> 42:16.246 align:middle
So it's really a
different thing.

42:17.016 --> 42:22.196 align:middle
You know, the investment
we're getting in equipment

42:22.306 --> 42:25.606 align:middle
and all those things that go
into creating data centers

42:25.606 --> 42:29.006 align:middle
and feeding the AI, it's
clearly one of the big sources

42:29.006 --> 42:30.016 align:middle
of growth in the economy.

42:30.606 --> 42:33.676 align:middle
Consumer spending also,
though, has been-is much bigger

42:33.676 --> 42:38.976 align:middle
than that and has been growing
and has defied a lot of,

42:39.286 --> 42:42.546 align:middle
you know, negative forecasts,
continuing to do so this year.

42:42.746 --> 42:43.896 align:middle
Consumers are still spending.

42:43.946 --> 42:47.206 align:middle
Now, it is-it may be
mostly higher-end consumers

42:47.376 --> 42:49.106 align:middle
or maybe skewed that way,

42:49.476 --> 42:51.156 align:middle
but the consumer
spending-and that's a big,

42:51.196 --> 42:53.276 align:middle
big chunk of what's
going on in the economy,

42:53.326 --> 42:56.026 align:middle
bigger than-substantially
bigger than AI.

42:56.026 --> 43:00.916 align:middle
You could point to growth-I
mean, actually, maybe-growth

43:00.916 --> 43:02.006 align:middle
as opposed to level,

43:02.256 --> 43:04.486 align:middle
but consumer spending is a much
bigger part of the economy.

43:05.106 --> 43:05.916 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

43:05.916 --> 43:07.876 align:middle
And why do you think that the
labor market is slowing so much,

43:07.916 --> 43:09.376 align:middle
even though consumer
spending is strong?

43:09.596 --> 43:10.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:10.496 --> 43:11.426 align:middle
Why has it slowed so much?

43:11.956 --> 43:14.606 align:middle
Well, what's happened
is that the supply

43:14.606 --> 43:18.066 align:middle
of workers has dropped
very, very sharply due

43:18.066 --> 43:24.016 align:middle
to mainly immigration, but also
lower labor force participation.

43:24.226 --> 43:27.246 align:middle
And that means there's
less need for new jobs,

43:27.486 --> 43:32.846 align:middle
because there's-there isn't
this flow into the pool of labor

43:32.946 --> 43:36.886 align:middle
where people need jobs because
there aren't those people now,

43:36.886 --> 43:40.156 align:middle
so there's not a supply of
workers showing up for jobs.

43:40.446 --> 43:43.066 align:middle
In addition, demand
has also gone down

43:43.066 --> 43:45.706 align:middle
and so has-labor force
participation has declined,

43:45.706 --> 43:48.236 align:middle
which is more of a
sign, in this case,

43:48.376 --> 43:51.046 align:middle
of demand as well as trend.

43:51.586 --> 43:53.846 align:middle
So I think you're
seeing some softening.

43:54.596 --> 43:56.836 align:middle
The economy is growing
at a slower rate

43:56.836 --> 43:58.836 align:middle
than it was-2.4 percent
last year,

43:58.836 --> 44:01.006 align:middle
we think around 1.6
percent this year.

44:01.006 --> 44:03.606 align:middle
It could have been a
couple of tenths higher

44:03.756 --> 44:04.836 align:middle
if not for the shutdown.

44:05.026 --> 44:07.316 align:middle
And, of course, that
will reverse,

44:07.536 --> 44:11.546 align:middle
but you've still got the economy
growing at a moderate pace.

44:11.986 --> 44:14.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:16.706 --> 44:17.816 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

44:17.816 --> 44:20.516 align:middle
Hey there-Andrew Ackerman
with the Washington Post.

44:20.776 --> 44:23.686 align:middle
I wanted to ask if you could
elaborate on how you think

44:23.686 --> 44:26.436 align:middle
about policy in the
context of the data drought.

44:26.776 --> 44:29.876 align:middle
Does it make you inclined to
stick with your plans as set

44:29.876 --> 44:32.026 align:middle
out in September in
the absence of the data

44:32.026 --> 44:34.456 align:middle
that might change your mind,
or does it make you inclined

44:34.456 --> 44:37.026 align:middle
to proceed with added caution
because of uncertainty?

44:37.116 --> 44:37.386 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:38.236 --> 44:40.866 align:middle
Yeah, well, we'll
know when we face

44:40.866 --> 44:42.466 align:middle
that question-if we
face that question.

44:43.206 --> 44:45.746 align:middle
I-it'll probably be
argued both ways, right?

44:45.816 --> 44:47.876 align:middle
But I've said a couple
times here

44:48.826 --> 44:51.816 align:middle
that if you-if you really
aren't getting information,

44:51.816 --> 44:55.386 align:middle
you really don't know, and the
economy looks like it's solid

44:55.386 --> 44:57.096 align:middle
and stable and hasn't
really changed,

44:57.546 --> 44:58.936 align:middle
there will be an argument,

44:58.936 --> 45:00.736 align:middle
I don't know how
persuasive it will be,

45:00.736 --> 45:03.926 align:middle
but there will be an
argument that you slow

45:03.926 --> 45:08.736 align:middle
down in a-when you
can't see as far ahead.

45:09.276 --> 45:12.826 align:middle
Others may argue-I'm not sure
where this argument will come,

45:12.826 --> 45:14.856 align:middle
but you're perfectly right
that you could also argue,

45:15.666 --> 45:16.866 align:middle
things haven't really changed.

45:16.986 --> 45:18.346 align:middle
But you might not know that,

45:18.346 --> 45:20.666 align:middle
so I don't know whether
we'll face this or not.

45:20.666 --> 45:21.336 align:middle
I hope we don't.

45:21.846 --> 45:23.576 align:middle
I hope by the time of
the December meeting,

45:23.576 --> 45:26.256 align:middle
we're getting a better
flow of data.

45:26.576 --> 45:27.566 align:middle
But if we-we're going to have

45:27.616 --> 45:28.966 align:middle
to do our jobs one
way or the other.

45:29.476 --> 45:29.986 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

45:29.986 --> 45:32.036 align:middle
Okay. I also just wanted
to ask a FinReg question.

45:32.086 --> 45:34.376 align:middle
I think several years ago,
you said the overall amount

45:34.376 --> 45:36.226 align:middle
of capital in the
system was about right.

45:36.526 --> 45:39.916 align:middle
As the Fed moves forward with
a revised Basel proposal,

45:40.116 --> 45:42.506 align:middle
potentially changes to
the G-SIB surcharge,

45:42.836 --> 45:44.596 align:middle
has that changed at all?

45:45.076 --> 45:47.856 align:middle
Or are you planning to
significantly reduce the amount

45:47.856 --> 45:48.826 align:middle
of capital in the system?

45:49.326 --> 45:50.726 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

45:50.726 --> 45:55.706 align:middle
So there's discussions going
on, I think, among the agencies,

45:56.126 --> 45:59.326 align:middle
and I don't want to get
ahead of those discussions.

45:59.456 --> 46:02.006 align:middle
I continue to think that
the level of capital,

46:02.006 --> 46:04.736 align:middle
when I said that in
2020, was about right.

46:05.126 --> 46:08.056 align:middle
And, of course, there's been
much capital added since then,

46:08.116 --> 46:09.386 align:middle
through various mechanisms,

46:10.246 --> 46:16.986 align:middle
but I look forward to-I know
these discussions are really

46:16.986 --> 46:18.866 align:middle
just getting going, they
haven't come to the point

46:18.866 --> 46:21.556 align:middle
where they're-where
they've got a whole plan

46:21.556 --> 46:22.376 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

46:22.376 --> 46:25.016 align:middle
So I really don't have
much to say on that.

46:25.016 --> 46:25.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:26.186 --> 46:27.586 align:middle
Brian. BRIAN CHEUNG.

46:28.186 --> 46:29.056 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

46:29.056 --> 46:30.776 align:middle
Brian Cheung with NBC News.

46:31.316 --> 46:34.786 align:middle
Is the weakness in the
jobs market accelerating,

46:35.046 --> 46:36.126 align:middle
and who's at risk

46:36.126 --> 46:39.246 align:middle
if the interest rate cuts are
not the effective medicine

46:39.246 --> 46:41.636 align:middle
for a further slowdown
in the labor market?

46:41.636 --> 46:42.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:42.056 --> 46:45.416 align:middle
So we do not see the weakness,
as you put it, the weakness

46:45.416 --> 46:46.736 align:middle
in the job market accelerating.

46:47.266 --> 46:49.046 align:middle
I would say, then again,

46:49.046 --> 46:51.826 align:middle
we don't have-we didn't get the
September employment report,

46:52.626 --> 46:57.806 align:middle
payroll report, but
we do get-we look

46:58.616 --> 47:01.096 align:middle
at the unemployment
insurance claims,

47:01.096 --> 47:03.586 align:middle
initial claims are
reported at the state level,

47:03.586 --> 47:04.466 align:middle
and we still get those.

47:04.496 --> 47:06.516 align:middle
And we can cumulate them
and get the national number,

47:06.516 --> 47:08.266 align:middle
and there's really-I
mean, there's no story.

47:08.266 --> 47:09.206 align:middle
You can look at the numbers.

47:09.436 --> 47:11.696 align:middle
Same thing with the job
openings we get from Indeed.

47:12.256 --> 47:14.336 align:middle
There's just no story
over the last four weeks.

47:14.336 --> 47:15.486 align:middle
It's kind of stable.

47:15.686 --> 47:19.276 align:middle
So you don't see anything that
says that the job market-or,

47:19.276 --> 47:20.216 align:middle
really, any part

47:20.216 --> 47:23.916 align:middle
of the economy-is making a
significant deterioration.

47:24.346 --> 47:25.686 align:middle
Don't see that.

47:26.076 --> 47:27.796 align:middle
You see more things,
like I mentioned, though,

47:27.796 --> 47:31.856 align:middle
which is that you see big
companies making announcements

47:31.856 --> 47:35.126 align:middle
of either layoffs or of the idea

47:35.126 --> 47:37.566 align:middle
that they can-they
won't need to hire.

47:38.206 --> 47:41.366 align:middle
Their number of employees
is not expected to go

47:41.366 --> 47:43.316 align:middle
up over a number of years.

47:43.646 --> 47:45.826 align:middle
There may be different people
working there then-they say

47:45.826 --> 47:46.266 align:middle
that too.

47:46.626 --> 47:48.676 align:middle
But they don't need
a bigger head count.

47:48.676 --> 47:49.976 align:middle
So you see those
kinds of things.

47:50.766 --> 47:51.316 align:middle
You don't see it

47:51.316 --> 47:54.036 align:middle
in the aggregate numbers-the
layoff numbers have not gone up.

47:54.706 --> 47:57.676 align:middle
But job creation is very low.

47:58.316 --> 48:01.286 align:middle
And the job-finding
rate for people

48:01.286 --> 48:03.616 align:middle
who are unemployed is very low.

48:04.026 --> 48:06.166 align:middle
But the unemployment
rate is very low as well,

48:06.166 --> 48:09.256 align:middle
4.3 percent is a low
employment-unemployment rate.

48:10.146 --> 48:10.636 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

48:10.636 --> 48:13.106 align:middle
And so, as you make these cuts,
is it the lower-income workers

48:13.106 --> 48:13.946 align:middle
that you're thinking about,

48:13.946 --> 48:16.146 align:middle
or those who might have
their jobs automated?

48:16.146 --> 48:17.526 align:middle
Is there a particular-
CHAIR POWELL.

48:17.526 --> 48:18.506 align:middle
That we're thinking of?

48:18.506 --> 48:18.876 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

48:19.006 --> 48:20.776 align:middle
-section of the labor market

48:20.776 --> 48:23.516 align:middle
that you're particularly-
CHAIR POWELL.

48:23.586 --> 48:27.526 align:middle
So, we can't-our
tools don't allow us

48:27.526 --> 48:29.346 align:middle
to target any demographic
or any income level

48:29.346 --> 48:30.606 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

48:30.606 --> 48:34.826 align:middle
I do think that people-we saw
this during the Global Financial

48:35.366 --> 48:37.096 align:middle
Crisis and the long recovery.

48:37.096 --> 48:39.046 align:middle
If you have a long period

48:39.206 --> 48:43.216 align:middle
of very strong labor market
conditions, it benefits people

48:43.216 --> 48:44.086 align:middle
at the lower end the most.

48:44.086 --> 48:46.226 align:middle
The last two or three years
of that long expansion,

48:46.756 --> 48:48.036 align:middle
people at the lower part

48:48.036 --> 48:53.886 align:middle
of the income spectrum were
getting the biggest benefits,

48:54.426 --> 48:57.206 align:middle
and lots of things were
happening in that space,

48:57.206 --> 49:01.306 align:middle
demographically, which
were very constructive.

49:01.306 --> 49:03.126 align:middle
And we're not at
that place right now.

49:03.126 --> 49:07.496 align:middle
But so, yeah, a stronger labor
market is the best thing we can

49:07.496 --> 49:09.206 align:middle
do for the public.

49:09.206 --> 49:10.266 align:middle
It's also part of our job.

49:10.266 --> 49:11.396 align:middle
It is half of our job.

49:11.396 --> 49:15.986 align:middle
But it is absolutely the best
thing we can do for people,

49:16.096 --> 49:17.906 align:middle
along with keeping
prices stable.

49:19.386 --> 49:20.956 align:middle
Those two things.

49:21.506 --> 49:23.816 align:middle
I mean, inflation
also hurts people

49:23.816 --> 49:26.186 align:middle
on fixed incomes more
than other people.

49:26.186 --> 49:27.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:27.106 --> 49:27.526 align:middle
Courtenay.

49:27.596 --> 49:27.996 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

49:28.076 --> 49:30.246 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

49:30.246 --> 49:32.896 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

49:32.896 --> 49:36.936 align:middle
As you know, the 12 Reserve
Bank presidents' terms are

49:36.936 --> 49:39.196 align:middle
up for renewal at
the end of February.

49:39.196 --> 49:43.416 align:middle
I'm wondering if you
can give us a timeline

49:43.416 --> 49:47.096 align:middle
for when the Board will
consider those reappointments

49:47.096 --> 49:49.746 align:middle
and whether we can expect
everyone to be re-upped

49:49.746 --> 49:52.576 align:middle
or if we can expect
some changes.

49:52.576 --> 49:53.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:53.736 --> 49:57.286 align:middle
So it's a process that we
go through, under the law.

49:57.476 --> 49:59.926 align:middle
The way the law works is,

50:00.146 --> 50:07.376 align:middle
we have a reappointment
process every five years for all

50:07.466 --> 50:10.126 align:middle
of the-all of the Reserve-each
of them and all of them.

50:10.126 --> 50:15.726 align:middle
We're in the middle of that
process, and we're going

50:16.556 --> 50:18.626 align:middle
to complete it in a timely way.

50:18.626 --> 50:20.856 align:middle
And I really-that's
really all I can say.

50:21.546 --> 50:22.466 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

50:22.466 --> 50:23.986 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

50:24.566 --> 50:25.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:25.206 --> 50:27.246 align:middle
Daniel. DANIEL AVIS.

50:27.246 --> 50:30.556 align:middle
Daniel Avis, Agence
France-Presse.

50:30.816 --> 50:35.476 align:middle
You now have dissents in three
consecutive FOMC meetings,

50:35.506 --> 50:38.366 align:middle
but in this meeting
you had dissents going

50:39.676 --> 50:41.266 align:middle
in both directions.

50:41.266 --> 50:46.206 align:middle
And you just mentioned
the strong disagreement

50:46.806 --> 50:49.496 align:middle
over the future path
of rate cuts.

50:49.496 --> 50:55.586 align:middle
Have these disagreements
complicated your role presiding

50:55.586 --> 50:57.766 align:middle
over the FOMC meetings?

50:57.846 --> 50:58.936 align:middle
And, if so, how?

50:59.026 --> 50:59.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:59.556 --> 51:00.656 align:middle
I wouldn't say that, no.

51:00.716 --> 51:04.676 align:middle
I think you-you have
to take what comes,

51:04.676 --> 51:08.806 align:middle
and what comes right now is a
pretty challenging situation

51:09.766 --> 51:15.216 align:middle
where we have-first of all, we
have 4.3 percent unemployment,

51:15.216 --> 51:18.086 align:middle
we have an economy that's
growing close to 2 percent.

51:18.086 --> 51:20.186 align:middle
So, overall, it's
a good picture.

51:20.186 --> 51:20.936 align:middle
It's a good picture.

51:20.936 --> 51:25.216 align:middle
But in terms of our policy, we
have upside risks to inflation,

51:25.216 --> 51:27.216 align:middle
downside risks to employment.

51:27.816 --> 51:31.646 align:middle
And this is a very difficult
thing for a central bank,

51:31.716 --> 51:36.496 align:middle
because one of those calls
for rates to be lower,

51:36.606 --> 51:38.996 align:middle
one calls for rates
to be higher.

51:39.946 --> 51:41.106 align:middle
We can't do both.

51:41.106 --> 51:47.616 align:middle
So we have to balance the two,
and so it's a challenging thing.

51:47.616 --> 51:49.426 align:middle
And as we have worked our
way through this process,

51:49.426 --> 51:53.606 align:middle
you would expect that there
would be a range of views

51:53.606 --> 51:56.996 align:middle
across the Committee on
what to do and the speed

51:57.176 --> 51:58.966 align:middle
with which we should do it.

51:58.966 --> 52:00.436 align:middle
And that's what we have.

52:00.436 --> 52:01.366 align:middle
That makes a lot of sense to me.

52:01.416 --> 52:04.026 align:middle
These are-all of these
people are people

52:04.026 --> 52:07.866 align:middle
who take their jobs very
seriously, work very hard

52:07.866 --> 52:09.406 align:middle
at this, and just want
to do what's right

52:09.406 --> 52:11.426 align:middle
for the American people.

52:11.536 --> 52:14.716 align:middle
But they have different
views on what that is.

52:14.806 --> 52:19.626 align:middle
And it's an honor to work among
people who care that much.

52:19.626 --> 52:22.426 align:middle
It is. But I don't
feel that it's unfair

52:22.426 --> 52:23.576 align:middle
or anything like that.

52:23.576 --> 52:31.506 align:middle
It's just-it's a time when we're
making quite difficult judgments

52:31.576 --> 52:35.276 align:middle
in real time, and I believe
we'll get through this,

52:35.276 --> 52:36.586 align:middle
and I think we've done sort

52:36.586 --> 52:40.206 align:middle
of the right things
so far this year.

52:40.206 --> 52:45.446 align:middle
I think we were-it
was appropriate for us

52:45.446 --> 52:47.816 align:middle
to be careful about this.

52:47.816 --> 52:52.576 align:middle
I think it would not be
appropriate to just ignore

52:52.576 --> 52:57.456 align:middle
or assume away the
inflation issue.

52:57.456 --> 52:59.046 align:middle
At the same time, I
think the risk of higher,

52:59.046 --> 53:02.776 align:middle
more persistent inflation
has declined significantly

53:02.776 --> 53:03.726 align:middle
since April.

53:03.726 --> 53:08.786 align:middle
And if we do wind up resuming
rate cuts at some point-we will

53:08.786 --> 53:12.656 align:middle
at some point-I think we're
trying to get to the end

53:12.736 --> 53:16.966 align:middle
of this cycle with the
labor market in a good place

53:17.066 --> 53:24.066 align:middle
and with inflation on its way
to 2 percent or at 2 percent.

53:24.066 --> 53:29.946 align:middle
So that's all we're trying
to do, and we're doing it

53:30.916 --> 53:33.626 align:middle
under quite challenging
circumstances

53:33.676 --> 53:35.636 align:middle
and doing the best we can.

53:35.636 --> 53:36.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

53:36.526 --> 53:39.806 align:middle
We'll go to Jennifer,
for the last question.

53:40.176 --> 53:40.876 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

53:40.876 --> 53:42.456 align:middle
Thank you so much, Chair Powell.

53:42.456 --> 53:44.656 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

53:44.656 --> 53:47.106 align:middle
Both regional and large
banks have taken losses

53:47.286 --> 53:51.726 align:middle
on loans given delinquencies
on subprime auto loans.

53:52.436 --> 53:56.246 align:middle
JPMorgan's CEO, Jamie Dimon,

53:56.426 --> 53:58.416 align:middle
warned when you see
one cockroach,

53:58.416 --> 54:00.686 align:middle
there may be more likely.

54:00.836 --> 54:04.996 align:middle
I'm curious how the Fed is
looking at these loan losses

54:04.996 --> 54:07.936 align:middle
and if it poses risks
to the financial system

54:07.936 --> 54:09.956 align:middle
or the outlook for the economy.

54:09.956 --> 54:11.516 align:middle
Is it a warning sign?

54:11.516 --> 54:11.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:11.746 --> 54:15.076 align:middle
So, we're-obviously we watch
these things very carefully,

54:15.076 --> 54:16.296 align:middle
credit conditions
very carefully.

54:16.296 --> 54:18.736 align:middle
You're right-you've seen rising
defaults in subprime credit

54:18.736 --> 54:20.546 align:middle
for some time now, and
now you've seen a number

54:20.616 --> 54:25.056 align:middle
of subprime credit-automobile
credit institutions having

54:25.056 --> 54:29.376 align:middle
significant losses, and some
of those losses are now showing

54:29.376 --> 54:31.536 align:middle
up on the books of banks.

54:31.866 --> 54:34.236 align:middle
We're looking at it carefully,
we're paying close attention.

54:34.236 --> 54:36.646 align:middle
I don't see, at this point,
a broader credit issue.

54:36.646 --> 54:38.766 align:middle
It doesn't seem to be something
that has very broad application

54:38.796 --> 54:39.576 align:middle
across financial institutions.

54:39.606 --> 54:40.416 align:middle
But we're going to
be-we're going

54:40.446 --> 54:41.796 align:middle
to be monitoring this quite
carefully and making sure

54:41.826 --> 54:42.336 align:middle
that that is the case.

54:42.366 --> 54:42.786 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

54:42.816 --> 54:44.226 align:middle
And then, separately, many-you
yourself have said that we're

54:44.256 --> 54:45.126 align:middle
in a bifurcated economy
right now

54:45.156 --> 54:46.566 align:middle
with high-net-worth
individuals continuing to spend,

54:46.596 --> 54:47.376 align:middle
with the lower income
pulling back.

54:47.406 --> 54:48.696 align:middle
How much of consumer
spending continuing hinges

54:48.726 --> 54:49.656 align:middle
on the stock market
remaining strong?

54:49.686 --> 54:51.246 align:middle
In some odd way, does the market
help keep the economy buoyant?

54:51.276 --> 54:51.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:51.546 --> 54:52.836 align:middle
So there is some-there is
some relationship there, but,

54:52.866 --> 54:53.766 align:middle
remember, the more
wealth someone has,

54:53.796 --> 54:54.906 align:middle
the lower an additional
dollar of wealth matters.

54:54.936 --> 54:55.716 align:middle
So your marginal propensity

54:55.746 --> 54:56.736 align:middle
to consume declines
quite dramatically

54:56.766 --> 54:58.146 align:middle
as you reach-as you reach
levels of stock market wealth.

54:58.176 --> 54:59.316 align:middle
So the stock market-it
would affect spending

54:59.346 --> 55:00.126 align:middle
if the stock market went down,

55:00.156 --> 55:01.746 align:middle
but it wouldn't drop-it wouldn't
drop sharply unless there were

55:01.776 --> 55:02.796 align:middle
quite a sharp drop
in the stock market.

55:02.826 --> 55:03.666 align:middle
People at the lower
end of income

55:03.696 --> 55:04.956 align:middle
and wealth have a much
higher marginal propensity

55:04.986 --> 55:06.366 align:middle
to consume an incremental
dollar of income or wealth,

55:06.396 --> 55:07.446 align:middle
but they don't have the
stock market wealth.

55:07.476 --> 55:08.946 align:middle
So I think it's certainly a
factor supporting consumption

55:08.976 --> 55:09.786 align:middle
right now, but-and
you would see it

55:09.816 --> 55:10.926 align:middle
if you saw material
corrections in spending.

55:10.956 --> 55:12.156 align:middle
But it wouldn't be-you
shouldn't think that it'll,

55:12.186 --> 55:13.056 align:middle
dollar for dollar,
stop consumption,

55:13.086 --> 55:13.866 align:middle
because that wouldn't
be the case.

55:13.896 --> 55:14.316 align:middle
Thanks very much.

